# Milestone: M3
# Covers: a long `|`-chain (many sequential statements) triggers a soft yield
#         when the per-strand safepoint_budget_remaining reaches zero; the
#         VM yields the strand back to the tail of the FIFO ready queue so
#         other strands can run, then resumes at the next instruction on the
#         next dispatch turn (row 12 §3.2 budget-exhaustion safepoint).
#
# blocked: a `|` chain is not a statement sequence the parser accepts
#          inside a body.  Probed: `{ 1 | 2 | 3 }` is a parse error, so
#          the long chain this fixture is named for cannot be written.
#          The budget-exhaustion claim underneath it is now covered
#          without a chain, by scheduler/budget_step_exhausted.chk and
#          budget_soft_yield.chk, both activated at the re-foundation.
#
# Everything below this line predates the re-foundation: it names
# tasks and entry points that no longer exist, and its account of
# what stands in the way has been superseded by the `blocked:` line
# above, which was probed against this branch.  Kept for the
# original urbiscript it carries.
# matrix-row: scheduler-pipe
#
# Reason: the chk runner executes under urbi_vm_run which drives the entire REPL
# input as a single transient strand without going through urbi_step or the
# cooperative scheduler ready queue.  Budget-exhaustion soft yield is only
# observable via urbi_step (the VM-wide step_budget_remaining > 0 check in
# ustep.c).  The substantive single-strand budget path was covered by
# test_pipe_budget_exhaust.c (unit test, which drove via urbi_step) until the
# Phase 0 runtime-internals test cleanup; no C-level coverage today.
# A proper chk fixture needs the ## host: driver mechanism to call urbi_step
# with a small budget and assert URBI_STEP_RUNNING before completion.
#
# When M5 + urbi_step chk driver land, implement this by running:
#   # tunables: default
#   var i = 0 | while (i < 10000) { i = i + 1 }
#   i
#   --- expected output (loop completes across multiple urbi_step turns) ---
#   [00000001] 10000
